+#### Limit exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all priced against, or backed by, the same underlying currency
+(for example several futures margined in the same coin, or several pairs quoted in the same
+stablecoin), each individual trade may look fine in isolation, while the combined exposure to that
+one currency is much larger than intended. If that currency moves against you, all of those
+positions get hit at the same time.
+
+`max_currency_exposure` limits, per underlying (quote) currency, what share of your tradable capital
+may be committed to open trades at any one time. For example, `max_currency_exposure=0.5` ensures
+that no more than 50% of the tradable capital is ever committed to trades sharing the same quote
+currency.
+
+When opening (or increasing) a trade would push the combined exposure to its underlying currency
+over the configured limit, the stake amount is reduced to fit under the limit - following the same
+"reduce, but skip if too small" logic used for [minimum trade stake](#minimum-trade-stake) - or the
+trade is skipped entirely if that would leave too little stake to trade. The reason is logged the
+same way as other stake-sizing adjustments. This behaves identically in live/dry-run and in
+backtesting, since both use the same sizing logic.
+
+[Strategy Override](#parameters-in-the-strategy).
+
 #### Amend last stake amount
 
 Assuming we have the tradable balance of 1000 USDT, `stake_amount=400`, and `max_open_trades=3`.
